# qu_serial_ports_discovery.qu -- a lighter-weight, general-purpose
# companion to qu_serial_realtime.qu, focused specifically on
# `serial_ports()` discovery and on `serial_open`/`.write()`/`.close()` in
# isolation, WITHOUT the full real-time acquisition loop (that's already
# covered by qu_serial_realtime.qu).
#
# Hardware reality on this machine: `serial_ports()` genuinely reports real
# COM ports (COM1, COM3 at the time this was written). COM3 in particular
# is very likely a real display-panel controller rather than a generic
# loopback device, so this demo deliberately never `.write()`s arbitrary
# bytes to any REAL port it discovers -- it only opens and immediately
# closes each one (proving `serial_open` genuinely acquires the OS handle),
# and demonstrates the write/read ERROR paths against a port name that
# does not exist on this machine instead. That keeps this script safe to
# run on any machine with real serial hardware attached, honest about what
# it can prove without a loopback pair, and non-overlapping with the fuller
# real-time capture loop in qu_serial_realtime.qu.
#
# Constructs exercised (all real, verified-working builtins):
#   serial_ports()                    -- real OS enumeration; a List of Str port names
#   serial_open(port, baud, ...)      -- open a real port / a clear error for a bad one
#   close(conn)                       -- release the OS handle
#   write(conn, data) / conn.write()  -- error path against a never-open handle's port
#   try/catch                         -- graceful handling of both error paths

backend auto

#%% Discovery: `serial_ports()` returns a List of Str port names -- an
# empty list on a machine with no serial hardware is a correct, honest
# result, not an error (see serial_ops.rs's own doc comment).
ports = serial_ports()
print("serial_ports() found {length(ports)} port(s): {ports}")
if length(ports) > 0
    print("first entry: \"{ports[0]}\" (a plain Str, exactly like every other entry in the list)")
end if

#%% Real hardware, read-only touch: open and immediately close every real
# port this machine reports. No `.write()` and no `.read_line()` here --
# deliberately, since at least one of these (COM3 on this machine) is
# suspected real display-panel hardware, not a safe-to-poke loopback
# device. This still proves `serial_open`/`close` genuinely acquire and
# release a real OS handle for each one.
for i = 0 to length(ports) - 1
    port = ports[i]
    try
        conn = serial_open(port, 9600, timeout_ms=200)
        close(conn)
        print("  {port}: opened and closed cleanly")
    catch err
        print("  {port}: could not open ({err.message})")
    end
end for

#%% The error path: a port name that (almost certainly) does not exist on
# any machine. `serial_open` itself is where this fails -- there is no
# handle to `.write()` to at all, so the honest demonstration is that the
# OPEN attempt is rejected with a clear, actionable message naming the
# port, rather than a crash or a hang.
missing_port = "COM249"
already_taken = false
for i = 0 to length(ports) - 1
    if ports[i] == missing_port
        already_taken = true
    end if
end for
print("attempting to open a nonexistent port \"{missing_port}\" (already in real port list: {already_taken})")

try
    bad_conn = serial_open(missing_port, 9600, timeout_ms=200)
    write(bad_conn, "should never get here")
    close(bad_conn)
    print("  unexpectedly succeeded opening \"{missing_port}\" -- treat that as real hardware present")
catch err
    print("  serial_open(\"{missing_port}\", ...) failed as expected: {err.message}")
end
